 "grammar": {
  "title": "Present Perfect with For, Since, Just, Yet, Already",
  "explanation": "The **present perfect** connects the past to the present. We form it with **have** or **has** plus the past participle.\n\nUse **for** with a length of time. It answers the question how long — ten minutes, one hour, or three days.\n\nUse **since** with the starting point of time. It answers the question when it started — morning, Monday, or 2010.\n\n**Just** means the action finished very recently. It stands between **have** and the participle.\n\n**Already** shows something happened before now, often sooner than expected. It stands in the same middle position.\n\n**Yet** is for questions and negative sentences. It always goes at the very end.\n\nDo not mix **for** and **since**. **For** needs a length, and **since** needs a starting point.",
  "examples": [
   "I *have simmered* the soup for ten minutes. (a length of time)",
   "She *has roasted* chicken since 2010. (a starting point)",
   "I *have just chopped* the onions. (very recently)",
   "He *has already marinated* the chicken. (sooner than expected)",
   "Have you *stirred* the sauce *yet*? (a question)",
   "The meat is not tender *yet*. We *have not cooked* it long enough. (a negative sentence)"
  ],
